abstract: >-
  jyotirganita computes the elements of the Indian calendar (tithi,
  nakṣatra, yoga, karaṇa, saṅkrānti, the planets' longitudes and the
  eras) by the rules of the traditional astronomical texts and by modern
  astronomy from the JPL ephemerides. Version 1.0.0 carries the
  Āryabhaṭīya, Parahita, Dṛggaṇita and the Sūrya-siddhānta (in Burgess's
  translation and in Parameśvara's recension). The texts' constants are
  read from cited pages of printed editions, and the modern tables are
  taken from published sources recorded with their checksums. The text
  methods compute in exact arithmetic, and the tests check them against
  the worked examples printed in the texts and their commentaries, and
  against published tables. Regional conventions are kept in separate
  profiles, Kerala's the first. Each result records the engine version,
  the configuration and the data files it used, and its method's status.
  It is meant for the study of inscriptions and historical almanacs
  (pañcāṅgas).
